\pard\qc\b\fs28 1. Introduction\par
\pard\sa180\b0\fs24 This document presents the security documentation for DayungApp, a Flutter-based application supported by Supabase services for authentication, database management, storage, and protected server-side actions. The report is written to describe how the system currently protects user accounts, application data, administrative functions, and operational processes. It also identifies areas where security controls are already present and areas that still require additional hardening before the system can be considered mature from an enterprise security perspective.\par

\pard\b 1.1 Project Overview\par
\pard\sa180\b0 DayungApp is a digital management system designed around role-based workflows for Dayung units and organization members. The project contains a Flutter client under the main application codebase, a legacy Node.js backend kept for reference and possible SMS restoration, and Supabase-backed services that now handle most modern server responsibilities. Based on the codebase and documentation, the system supports several operational roles including superadmin, president, secretary, treasurer, collector, and member. These roles interact with system modules such as login, registration, membership applications, contribution and payment flows, claims, audit logs, system settings, and day-to-day notifications.\par

\pard\b 1.2 Purpose of the System\par
\pard\sa180\b0 The purpose of DayungApp is to centralize account management, membership processing, role-based administration, contribution tracking, claim-related workflows, and communication between organization officers and members. Because the application processes personally identifiable information, payment-related records, account credentials, uploaded documents, and administrative settings, security is a core requirement rather than an optional enhancement. The system must protect confidentiality, maintain data integrity, preserve service availability, and ensure that only authorized users can access privileged functions.\par

\pard\b 1.3 Intended Users\par
\pard\sa180\b0 The intended users of the system are grouped according to role. Superadmins manage global configuration, reports, user lifecycle actions, and protected system administration functions. Presidents, secretaries, treasurers, and collectors work within assigned Dayung units and perform operational tasks based on their respective responsibilities. Members use the system for account access, viewing records, submitting application or claim-related information, and monitoring organization transactions that apply to them. Since not all users should see the same records or perform the same actions, the system depends heavily on role separation and authorization controls.\par

\pard\b 1.4 Platform and Technology Used\par
\pard\sa180\b0 DayungApp uses Flutter as the main client framework, which allows the same codebase to support mobile and desktop targets. For backend capabilities, the project relies on Supabase Auth for authentication, Supabase PostgreSQL for application data, Supabase Storage for uploaded files, and Supabase Edge Functions for sensitive server-side operations. The repository also shows a legacy Express backend retained mainly for reference and possible SMS work, but privileged SuperAdmin functionality has been moved toward Supabase-native server execution. In addition, the codebase uses helper libraries and internal utilities for input sanitization, secure storage, connectivity handling, HTTP communication, and UI state management.\par

\pard\qc\b\fs28 2. Security Objectives and Requirements\par
\pard\sa180\b0 The primary security objective of DayungApp is to ensure that every user accesses only the data and features appropriate to their role. This means that the system must authenticate users before granting access, verify their authorization before allowing sensitive actions, and protect data both while it is being transmitted and while it is stored. Another important objective is traceability. Administrative actions such as account creation, password reset, disabling a user, login attempts, and major profile or system events should be logged so that misuse can be investigated.\par

\pard\sa180 The repository's security policy files define several concrete requirements. Passwords must be at least eight characters long and should contain uppercase, lowercase, numeric, and special characters. All users must authenticate through Supabase Auth. Privileged backend actions must verify an authenticated bearer token and confirm that the actor has proper role ownership. Logging must capture significant login and profile events, and production code should avoid ad hoc debugging output during sensitive operations. Operationally, secrets must stay out of source control, and backup, retention, and recovery procedures must be managed outside the mobile application itself.\par

\pard\qc\b\fs28 3. Authentication and Account Security\par
\pard\sa180\b0 Authentication in DayungApp is centered on Supabase Auth. This is the primary identity provider used by the application when users sign in. The current code also shows that the application contains bcrypt-based password hashing and verification logic in account-related flows, which indicates that password-related data handling has been implemented with stronger cryptographic practices than legacy custom hashing helpers. The documented policy explicitly states that legacy SHA-256 password helpers must not be used, which is an important corrective measure for reducing weak or inconsistent credential handling.\par

\pard\sa180 The login flow performs more than a simple username and password check. Based on the repository, it also checks runtime system conditions such as maintenance mode, records audit events, refreshes role-related information, and routes the user according to role after successful authentication. Password recovery and forced password change flows are also present in the project, which is a positive control because temporary or reset credentials should not remain unchanged indefinitely. In the current architecture, newly created or reset accounts can be flagged to require a password change, helping reduce the risk of long-lived temporary credentials.\par

\pard\sa180 From a security standpoint, these controls improve account protection in several ways. First, users are authenticated through a managed provider instead of through insecure client-only checks. Second, password reset and first-login change requirements reduce the exposure of default credentials. Third, audit logging for authentication-related events improves traceability. However, the system still depends on correct policy enforcement and proper deployment configuration, so strong authentication alone does not solve every access control issue.\par

\pard\qc\b\fs28 4. Authorization and Access Control\par
\pard\sa180\b0 Authorization in DayungApp is implemented through role-based access control. The roles used in the system include superadmin, president, secretary, treasurer, collector, and member. These roles are reflected both in application behavior and in backend expectations. Superadmins hold the highest privilege level and can perform protected administrative actions such as user lifecycle management, reporting, and system settings control. Operational officers are expected to act within their assigned Dayung units, while members should generally see only their own or unit-appropriate data.\par

\pard\sa180 The application documentation correctly states that client-side visibility rules are not a substitute for backend or database authorization. This is an important principle because simply hiding a button in the Flutter interface does not prevent a determined user from attempting an unauthorized request. To address this, the project uses Supabase Row Level Security and role-aware policies, and it also introduces Supabase Edge Functions for privileged functions that cannot safely run from the client. In particular, the superadmin-admin function is intended to handle actions such as creating users, resetting another user's password, disabling accounts, and reading disabled-aware reports. This design is stronger than performing those tasks directly from the client because it keeps highly privileged operations on the server side.\par

\pard\sa180 At the same time, the repository's RLS review shows that authorization is only partially hardened at present. The review notes that several permissive policies still use conditions equivalent to broad allow rules, which can reopen access that stricter policies were meant to restrict. Sensitive areas identified in the review include users, payments, claims, beneficiaries, audit logs, service checklist data, and storage objects. Therefore, while role segregation exists in the design and in many code paths, the current deployed policy posture should be described as partially implemented rather than fully complete.\par

